rately identify whether sensor data is indicative of normal and/or abnormal conditions"}, {"pat_id": "US-12252874-B2", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "method 300 begins at block 302, where drain sensor data is received from the drainage sensors (e.g., the set of drainage sensors 146a-d of figs"}, {"pat_id": "US-12252874-B2", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "at block 322, tidal backflow and no tidal backflow zones of the drainage sensor depth data may be determined"}, {"pat_id": "US-9709431-B1", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "in this example, if the rain sensor indicates that there is no local precipitation, but that there are water levels above a threshold, the control system 100 may track property operations to determine if there is a problematic operation that may be causing water leakage within the property"}, {"pat_id": "US-9709431-B1", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "in this example, if the rain sensor indicates that there is excessive local precipitation, the calculated susceptibility of water damage may be increased based on determining that the likelihood of water leaking into the property is much higher"}]</data>
